<spell-notebook "progress usage">
	<header level="1">
		Progress
		<header>A progress bar shows the progression of a task.</>
	</>
	<progress-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#task-completion">Task completion</>
			<item link="#custom-labels">Custom labels</>
			<item link="#events">Events</>
			<item link="#behaviors">Behaviors</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			<item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (numbers, labels, events), Fomantic's usage tab translated, accessibility, frameworks. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Write `<ui-progress>` with its numbers as attributes (or the matching properties), and the bar follows. There is
			no `.progress()` call to make: Fomantic's metadata (`data-percent`, `data-value`, `data-total`) and settings
			object are the attributes.

			- `value` is a share of `total`, or a percentage when there is no `total`; `percent` wins over both.
			- A comma list (`value="10,20,30"`) makes several bars; `bar-colors` gives each a hue.
			- `bar-text` puts `percent` (`45%`) or `ratio` (`9 of 20`, needs `total`) inside the bar; unset, none.
			- `label` (or the slotted text) is the line under the bar, and names the bar for assistive tech.
			- `precision` sets the decimal places shown in the texts.
			- `state` (`success`, `warning`, `error`) tints the bar and its label; unset, a single bar turns `success` at
			  100% by itself.
			- `active` is only the look, on while you set it: Fomantic's JS pulsed every bar between 0 and 100% by itself.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Task completion">
		<markdown>
			With a `total`, a bar keeps track of the current value as a ratio of the total: useful for a series of steps
			with a known count, like "uploading 1 of 20" photos. Each step is a write to `value`.
		</>

		<section label="Adding a total value" sticky collapsible dividing>
			<example description="`total` sets what `value` counts up to;  `bar-text=\"ratio\"` shows the count.">
				<progress indicating value="1" total="20" bar-text="ratio" label="Adding Photos"/>
			</example>
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const photos = document.querySelector("ui-progress[total]")
			photos.value = String(Number(photos.value) + 1) // Fomantic's .progress("increment")
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Custom labels">
		<markdown>
			`label` is a template: these words are filled in on every change.

			- `{percent}` -- current percentage
			- `{value}` -- current value
			- `{total}` -- total value
			- `{left}` -- distance to the total, or the percentage left
		</>

		<section label="Templated label" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example description="`label=\"Adding {value} of {total} photos\"`, filled in as the value changes.">
				<html>
					<ui-progress indicating value="7" total="20" label="Adding {value} of {total} photos"></ui-progress>
					<ui-progress value="12" total="20" label="{left} photos to go"></ui-progress>
				</html>
			</example>
		</>

		<section label="Translation" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example description="The label is yours, so write it in the page's language;  the bar texts (`45%`, `9 of 20`) are the vocabulary's `progressPercent` / `progressRatio` texts, which a translation replaces.">
				<html>
					<ui-progress value="15" total="20" label="{value} de {total}: carga de archivos"></ui-progress>
				</html>
			</example>
		</>
		<message size="small" class="site-not-ported"><html>Not ported: Fomantic's per-state label texts (text.active, text.success ...), and its automatic <code>label: 'ratio'</> switch. One <code>label</> template covers both:  swap it on <code>ui-completed</> when the finished bar should say something else.</></>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			Both fire after the first render, whatever wrote the numbers (there is no user input).

			- `ui-changed` -- the percentage changed; `detail` is `{ percent, percents, value?, total? }` (`percents` holds
			  one entry per bar).
			- `ui-completed` -- the percentage reached 100; `detail` is `{ value?, total? }`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const progress = document.querySelector("ui-progress")
			progress.addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => console.log(event.detail.percent))
			progress.addEventListener("ui-completed", () => console.log("done"))
			for (let done = 0; done <= 20; done++) progress.value = String(done) // with total="20"
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Behaviors">
		<markdown>
			Fomantic's behaviors are property writes here:

			- `set percent(percent)` -- `progress.percent = "45"` (or `"20,30,50"` for several bars)
			- `set progress(value)`, `update progress(value)` -- `progress.value = "9"`
			- `set total(total)` -- `progress.total = 20`
			- `increment` / `decrement` -- read `value`, write it back plus or minus a step
			- `complete` -- `progress.percent = "100"`
			- `set success` / `set warning` / `set error` -- `progress.state = "warning"`
			- `is complete` -- `progress.matches(":state(complete)")`
		</>
		<message size="small" class="site-not-ported">Not ported: the random increment step, the animation duration / updateInterval settings, and the onChange / onSuccess ... callbacks. The bar transitions in CSS, and the callbacks are the two events.</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The element itself is the `progressbar`, with `aria-valuemin`, `aria-valuemax` (the `total`, else 100) and
			  `aria-valuenow`; `aria-valuetext` reads like the bar text (`45%`, or `9 of 20`). An indeterminate bar has no
			  value, which assistive tech reads as "progress unknown".
			- Name every bar: `aria-label` on the host, or the `label` / slotted label text.
			- Colour is never the only signal: pair `state` with a label that says what happened.
			- The indeterminate and `active` animations stop under reduced motion.
			- If the element fails to render, the bars, their value, text and label go: only children written
			  inside the element still show, through a bare `<slot>`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>Numbers are plain attributes; listen for `ui-changed` / `ui-completed` like any custom event.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-progress value={done} total={20} bar-text="ratio" onui-complete={() => setFinished(true)} />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-progress :value="done" total="20" bar-text="ratio" @ui-completed="finished = true" />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-progress
			  value={done()}
			  total="20"
			  bar-text="ratio"
			  on-ui-completed={() => setFinished(true)}
			/>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
